content:
{
  "summary": "Tesla reports revenue under two XBRL concepts because it adopted the new ASC 606 revenue‑recognition standard (which introduced the concept ‘RevenueFromContractWithCustomerExcludingAssessedTax’) in its fiscal year 2018 filing, while still providing the legacy ‘Revenues’ concept for backward compatibility. For periods before the adoption (FY 2017 and earlier) the appropriate figure is the ‘Revenues’ concept; beginning with FY 2018 (the year ended December 31 2018) the ‘RevenueFromContractWithCustomerExcludingAssessedTax’ concept should be used as the primary revenue measure.",
  "claims": [
    "Tesla’s FY 2017 Form 10‑K (filed 2018‑02‑28) reports revenue only under the XBRL concept ‘Revenues’ with a value of $11,759,000,000.",
    "Tesla’s FY 2018 Form 10‑K (filed 2019‑02‑28) includes both concepts: ‘Revenues’ $21,461,000,000 and ‘RevenueFromContractWithCustomerExcludingAssessedTax’ $21,461,000,000, reflecting the adoption of ASC 606 effective for that year.",
    "From FY 2019 onward (e.g., FY 2019 Form 10‑K filed 2020‑02‑26 showing ‘RevenueFromContractWithCustomerExcludingAssessedTax’ $24,558,000,000) Tesla continues to report revenue under the newer concept as the primary figure."
  ]
}
